{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793885569,"nonce":"tgAlgFRIBsIjQn8JhAXe5rCeR5I=","message":"Owner asked whether mobile accessibility can be tested in emulators. Verified against current primary Android/Apple documentation: yes for substantial implementation and regression coverage. Android emulator can run TalkBack with Android Accessibility Suite installed (Google Play-capable image or suite already present), covering traversal, announcements, roles/states and assistive actions. iOS Simulator supports Accessibility Inspector and XCTest performAccessibilityAudit checks; actual iOS VoiceOver is unavailable in Simulator and needs physical-device validation. Emulator/audit success must not be recorded as a real-device VoiceOver pass.\n\nBlinc source audit still finds no mobile AccessibilityNodeProvider/virtual descendants or UIAccessibilityElement/container implementation for individual GPU-rendered controls in the current hosts. First implement the semantic tree/native adapter and action routing, then use emulator/simulator tests for roles, names, values/states, stable IDs/bounds, focus order, overlays and activation/value changes. Retain physical iOS VoiceOver validation as a separate release check. No emulator test or accessibility implementation is claimed by this research update.\n\nPrimary sources: https://developer.android.com/codelabs/basic-android-kotlin-compose-test-accessibility ; https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/ui/accessibility/views/custom-views ; https://developer.apple.com/documentation/accessibility/performing-accessibility-audits-for-your-app ; https://developer.apple.com/documentation/accessibility/performing-accessibility-testing-for-your-app","files":null}]}